<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/expert/international-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Domestic law has police, prisons and a sovereign; international law has none of these — and
    yet aircraft land on schedule under its rules, ambassadors are protected by them, most wars
    end within them, and ~90% of state conduct that international law addresses is compliant
    (Henkin's famous formulation). The discipline's real subject is therefore not a list of rules
    but a mechanism: <strong>how law can function without a central enforcer</strong> — through
    reciprocity, reputation, domestic institutions and the mutual interest of 195 states in an
    orderly world. Once you see the mechanism, both the system's successes (trade, oceans, human
    rights machinery) and its celebrated failures (enforcement against great powers) become
    legible as the same design operating at different stakes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the sources, the courts, and the compliance mystery" widget="equationStep" config={intlCfg} />

  <h2>Sources: where the law comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Article 38 of the ICJ Statute is the field's constitution, and its list organises everything.
    <strong>Treaties</strong> are contracts between states: negotiation, signature, ratification,
    binding under <em>pacta sunt servanda</em> (Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969)
    — from bilateral tax accords to the UN Charter, whose Article 2(4) prohibition on the use of
    force is the system's foundational rule. Treaty interpretation is a codified craft (VCLT
    Articles 31–32: ordinary meaning in context, in light of object and purpose — text plus
    telos, not will-hunting). <strong>Customary international law</strong> binds without
    signature: two elements, consistent <em>state practice</em> and <em>opinio juris</em> (the
    conviction that the practice is legally required, not merely convenient) — the source of the
    law of the sea before UNCLOS, diplomatic immunity, and the core of the law of armed
    conflict. The <strong>persistent objector</strong> rule polices the boundary (a state that
    consistently objected during formation is not bound); <strong>jus cogens</strong> norms —
    genocide, slavery, torture, aggression, the poison of racial discrimination — sit above the
    treaty system entirely, non-derogable and binding all. <strong>General principles of law</strong>
    (good faith, estoppel, res judicata — borrowed from domestic systems to fill gaps) complete
    the formal sources; <strong>soft law</strong> (declarations, guidelines, SDGs) is the
    system's normative weather — not binding, but the discourse from which future custom
    precipitates.
  </p>

  <h2>The compliance mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    The puzzle first: why obey at all? The layered answer is the field's core theory.
    <strong>Reciprocity</strong> does the daily work: diplomatic immunity, consular access,
    fishing zones and treaty performance are exchange relationships where one violation licenses
    another — the system's built-in tit-for-tat. <strong>Reputation</strong> is the currency:
    states are repeat players before markets, allies, creditors and courts; a rule-violator pays
    in credibility (Guzman's rational-choice account formalises this — compliance is an
    investment in future dealings). <strong>Domestic institutions</strong> internalise the rules:
    treaty obligations implemented by courts and legislatures (the dualism/monism machinery)
    generate compliance as a by-product of ordinary governance, and mobilise domestic
    constituencies who sue their own governments — the European human-rights system runs on
    exactly this. <strong>Vertical legitimacy</strong> and managerial habits (Chayes and Chayes)
    complete the picture: most non-compliance is incapacity and ambiguity, not defiance, and
    treaty bodies manage rather than punish. The exceptions define the limit: where stakes are
    existential or identity-laden (great-power security, territory, regime survival), the
    mechanism's costs exceed its grip — which is why compliance is strongest in technical,
    recursive, low-visibility domains and weakest exactly where the headlines look.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "international law isn't really law." The objection (Austin's
    command theory: no sovereign, no law) is a definition, not an argument — and it fails
    empirically: states <em>invoke</em> international law to justify and accuse, argue about
    what it requires, comply in the overwhelming run of cases, and build institutions to apply
    it. A system that functions as law among its subjects is law; the interesting question was
    never "is it law?" but "when does it bite?" — and the answer (reciprocity, reputation,
    stakes) is quantitative, not rhetorical.
  </p>

  <h2>Institutions: courts without police</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>International Court of Justice</strong> settles state-vs-state disputes
    (jurisdiction by consent — special agreements, treaty clauses; no compulsory jurisdiction),
    and its record is better than its reputation: compliance with adverse judgments is
    substantial, and where it fails the reason is usually Security Council politics (the
    <em>Nicaragua</em> precedent: the US, held liable for supporting the contras, vetoed
    enforcement — the system's limits demonstrated in one case). The <strong>International
    Criminal Court</strong> (Rome Statute, 2002) prosecutes <em>individuals</em> for genocide,
    crimes against humanity, war crimes and aggression — a genuine legal revolution (Nuremberg's
    legacy institutionalised), with an equally genuine structural flaw: major powers (US, Russia,
    China, India) are not parties, the Security Council can refer or defer, and prosecutions
    skew toward the weak — the selectivity critique (Africa-focused early docket) is the court's
    permanent legitimacy problem. The <strong>WTO dispute-settlement system</strong> was the
    crown jewel — binding adjudication of trade disputes with authorised retaliation as the
    enforcement tooth — until the Appellate Body's paralysis (US blockage of appointments since
    2019) demonstrated that even the most judicialised regime survives only while its members
    consent; the interim MPIA arrangement is the patch. The <strong>European Court of Human
    Rights</strong> is the strongest enforcement machine in international law: individual
    petition, binding judgments, and domestic courts that execute them — a regional system whose
    effectiveness (and current legitimacy battles with member governments) frames every
    discussion of what a global version would require. <strong>State responsibility</strong> is
    the connective law of the whole system: the ILC Articles codify when a state's breach
    triggers duties of cessation and reparation, and what lawful <strong>countermeasures</strong>
    (self-help short of force) the injured state may take.
  </p>

  <h2>The regimes that carry the system</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Human rights law</strong> grew from the Universal Declaration (1948 — technically
    non-binding, culturally foundational) into binding covenants (ICCPR, ICESCR, 1966), regional
    courts and treaty bodies with review procedures — a regime that transformed sovereignty by
    making <em>how states treat their own people</em> an international concern. Its critiques
    are structural: universality against cultural-relativist objection, indivisibility against
    the civil/political versus economic/social sorting, and enforcement that runs on naming and
    shaming more than coercion. <strong>The law of armed conflict</strong> (Geneva Conventions
    1949, universal ratification; the Hague rules; Additional Protocols) regulates war's conduct:
    distinction (civilians never targets), proportionality, precaution, humane treatment — law
    that assumes war will happen and constrains it, with the ICC and ad hoc tribunals (Yugoslavia,
    Rwanda) converting its gravest violations into individual criminal liability.
    <strong>Law of the sea</strong> (UNCLOS 1982, the "constitution for the oceans") allocates
    maritime zones from territorial sea to deep seabed, with compulsory dispute settlement and
    its own tribunal (ITLOS) — the model of a technical regime quietly governing a third of the
    planet. <strong>Trade, environment, climate</strong> complete the map: the WTO's legalised
    reciprocity, the climate regime's treaty-plus-custom architecture — where the ICJ's
    advisory opinion on climate obligations (2025, requested by a UN General Assembly vote
    secured by small island states and a youth-led campaign) held that states owe binding
    customary-law due-diligence duties to cut emissions (with 1.5 °C as the primary goal), and
    that failure to meet them is an internationally wrongful act triggering cessation and
    reparation — customary law, built from universal practice and concern, applied to the
    era's defining problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Law of the sea and space: regimes at the frontier</h2>
  <p>
    Two regimes show the system's range. <strong>UNCLOS</strong> (1982, near-universal)
    allocates the oceans: 12-nautical-mile territorial seas (full sovereignty), 200-mile
    exclusive economic zones (resource rights), the continental shelf, high-seas freedoms,
    and the deep seabed as "common heritage of mankind" (the International Seabed Authority —
    the system's most ambitiously communitarian institution, currently stressed by
    deep-sea-mining licensing pressure); compulsory dispute settlement (the International
    Tribunal for the Law of the Sea and UNCLOS annex tribunals) has produced real rulings
    against major powers — the South China Sea arbitration (2016), whose rejection by China
    does not erase the ruling's legal effect. <strong>Outer space</strong> (the Outer Space
    Treaty 1967) prohibits national appropriation, holds states responsible for national
    activities including private ones, and keeps lunar resources under a non-appropriation
    cloud that the Artemis Accords and commercial actors are now testing — space law is the
    regime whose moment is arriving exactly as its founding assumptions age. Both regimes
    show the system's pattern: technical rules, self-interested compliance (every state wants
    orderly seas and orbits), institutions for the disputes, and politics at the edges.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fragmentation.</strong> The system has no hierarchy of regimes: trade law, human
    rights law, environmental law and security law each grew their own treaties, courts and
    doctrines, and they collide (a trade measure that is also an environmental necessity; a
    sanctions regime against a human-rights violator that breaches trade commitments) — the
    ILC's fragmentation report named the problem; the WTO–environment interface (the
    <em>shrimp-turtle</em> line of cases, and the current climate-measure disputes) is where the
    collision is most live. <strong>Legitimacy and power.</strong> The system's rules were
    largely written by the powerful (the Security Council's permanent five; the Bretton Woods
    quotas), and its enforcement asymmetry — vigorous against the weak, discretionary against
    the strong — is the standing charge; the counter-argument is comparative: no alternative
    architecture has ever outperformed reciprocity-based order, and the post-1945 rules-bound
    era's great-power peace (however punctuated) is a data point in the rules' favour.
    <strong>The frontier domains.</strong> Cyber operations (does the non-intervention principle
    apply? what counts as an "attack"? the Tallinn Manual process is doctrine-in-construction);
    autonomous weapons and the meaning of "distinction" without a human in the loop; space
    resource exploitation under the Outer Space Treaty's non-appropriation rule; and
    geoengineering governance (a planetary intervention with no regime). <strong>Open
    questions:</strong> can the climate-advisory line of authority convert into reparations for
    loss and damage without collapsing into unpayable sums (the causation-and-apportionment
    problem at planetary scale); will the ICC's jurisdiction stretch to the powerful (the
    Palestine, Ukraine and Afghanistan dockets are the live tests); does the WTO survive its
    Appellate Body crisis as a judicial system or devolve into power-based bargaining; and —
    the discipline's oldest question with newest stakes — whether an order built for
    reciprocal state interests can govern problems (pandemics, AI, climate) whose harms and
    benefits are neither reciprocal nor purely interstate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The compliance literature's findings deserve their quantitative summary, because they
    answer the chapter's opening puzzle with evidence. Compliance rates across treaty regimes
    are high by any domestic comparison: trade obligations performed near-universally in
    dispute-settlement practice, aviation and telecommunications treaties functioning for
    decades as pure coordination, and even human-rights treaty reporting (criticised as
    ritual) showing measurable behavioural effects where domestic constituencies can
    litigate. The variance is the finding: compliance tracks domestic institutionalisation —
    courts, implementing legislation, mobilised publics — more than regime strength (the
    transnational legal process account: internalisation through interaction, interpretation
    and friction). The design implication drives the modern field: build institutions that
    empower domestic actors rather than relying on international enforcement — the European
    human-rights model's deepest lesson, now the template every new regime (climate, AI,
    pandemic governance) argues about.
  </p>
  <p>
    The teaching point for the whole course: international law is the limit case that
    clarifies what law is everywhere. Strip the sovereign and you see the machinery plainly —
    coordination, commitment devices, reputational markets, internalised norms — that
    domestic systems run with police uniforms over the top. The student who understands why
    states obey here understands, more sharply than any domestic course can teach, why
    obedience is never the whole story of law anywhere.
  </p>

